# Sanyo "Basic Serial Command" Projector (RS-232 serial)
#
# Controls projectors that speak Sanyo's "Basic Serial Command" RS-232
# protocol (the compact "C-command" scheme). Sanyo built projectors for
# several brands, so the same protocol shows up across:
#   - Sanyo PLC series (PLC-WM5500, PLC-ZM5000, PLC-XF1000, PLC-XF47K,
#     PLC-WL2500, PLC-XU305/355, ...)
#   - EIKI EK / LC series (EK-307W, EK-308U, EK-309W, EK-1000LU, ...)
#   - Boxlight (Eco, Boston, ProjectoWrite lines)
# Usable on a direct serial port or *through a bridge* (e.g. a Global Cache
# iTach IP2SL): bind it from the device's Connection settings and the platform
# routes it over the bridge's serial pass-through.
#
# Serial settings: 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit, no flow control.
# Baud and cable type VARY BY MODEL. 19200 is the common rate (configurable
# in settings); some models want a CROSS (null-modem) cable (e.g. EIKI
# EK-308U/309W), others a DIRECT (straight-through) cable (e.g. Boxlight Eco).
#
# Command format: ASCII "C" + a 2-character code, terminated with a single
# carriage return (CR, 0x0D). Two kinds:
#   - Functional execution (e.g. C00 = power on, C01 = power off). Projector
#     replies 0x06 (ACK) + CR on success, NAK + CR on failure; an unknown
#     command replies "?" + CR.
#   - Status read (e.g. CR0 = power status). Projector replies <value> + CR.
# This driver appends the CR; command "send" strings carry the trailing \r.
#
# The command surface here matches newer EIKI EK units. The core commands
# (power, basic inputs, blank, freeze, volume, brightness, menu, navigation,
# status) are common to the whole family; a few inputs (HDMI 2/MHL, Network,
# Memory Viewer, USB Display) are EK-era extras that older Sanyo PLC units
# simply NAK.
#
# Status replies are bare values with no echo of the query, so the driver
# can't pattern-match every read unambiguously. It polls the reads whose
# reply formats are mutually distinguishable: power (2-digit code), input
# (text), lamp hours (5 digits), temperatures (decimals), model name. The
# screen-setting (CR4) and lamp-mode (CR7) reads also return 2-digit codes
# that collide with the power code under fire-and-forget polling, so they are
# not polled here (they would need correlated query/response, which the
# declarative runtime does not currently do).
#
# Hardware-validated against an EIKI EK-307W (CR5 model read returns
# "Coral-EK-307W"): two-way comms at 19200 8N1 over a Global Cache iTach
# IP2SL with a crossover cable. Power on/off, input/lamp/temperature reads,
# and the ACK reply all verified.

id: sanyo_serial_projector
name: Sanyo / EIKI / Boxlight Projector (RS-232 Serial)
manufacturer: Sanyo
category: projector
version: 1.0.0
author: OpenAVC
description: >-
  Controls Sanyo-built projectors that use Sanyo's "Basic Serial Command"
  RS-232 protocol: Sanyo PLC, EIKI EK / LC, and Boxlight (Eco / Boston /
  ProjectoWrite) lines. Works on a direct serial port or through a serial
  bridge. Power, input select, blank, freeze, image modes, audio, zoom,
  keystone, on-screen navigation, and power management, with polled power /
  input / lamp-hours / temperature / model status. Baud and cable type vary
  by model (commonly 19200; both configurable).
source_url: https://www.eiki.com/rs232-commands/
tags: [projector, rs232, rs-232, serial, sanyo, eiki, boxlight, plc, sanyo-serial, basic-serial, ek-307w, bridge]
verified: true
simulated: false
ports: []
compatible_models:
  - manufacturer: EIKI
    models:
      - EK-307W
    confidence: full
    notes: >-
      Hardware-validated: two-way RS-232 control confirmed against an EK-307W
      (CR5 model read returns "Coral-EK-307W") at 19200 8N1 over a Global Cache
      iTach IP2SL serial bridge with a crossover cable.
  - manufacturer: EIKI
    models:
      - EK-308U
      - EK-309W
      - EK-1000LU
      - EK-850LU
      - EK-1100LU
    confidence: untested
    notes: >-
      EIKI EK / LC series projectors using the Sanyo Basic Serial Command
      protocol (same C00 / CRx command set). Verify baud and cable type per
      model before relying on it.
  - manufacturer: Sanyo
    models:
      - PLC-WM5500
      - PLC-ZM5000
      - PLC-XF1000
      - PLC-XF47K
      - PLC-WL2500
      - PLC-XU305
      - PLC-XU355
    confidence: untested
    notes: >-
      Sanyo PLC series, the origin of this "Basic Serial Command" protocol.
      Core power / input / status commands are shared; a few EK-era inputs
      here do not apply. Verify baud and cable type per model.
  - manufacturer: Boxlight
    models:
      - Eco Series
      - Boston Series
      - ProjectoWrite
    confidence: untested
    notes: >-
      Boxlight (Sanyo-built) lines using the same C-command protocol. Note
      some Boxlight models (e.g. Eco Series) want a DIRECT cable rather than a
      crossover. Verify per model.

transport: serial
# Replies are CR-terminated; sent commands also carry a trailing CR (in the
# command "send" strings — the protocol requires a CR after every command).
delimiter: "\r"

help:
  overview: >-
    EIKI EK-series projector over RS-232 (the compact "C-command" protocol:
    "C" + a 2-character code + carriage return). Works on a direct serial
    port or through a serial bridge (choose "Through a bridge" in Connection
    settings and pick the bridge's RS-232 port). Power, inputs, blank,
    freeze, image modes, audio, zoom, keystone, navigation, power management,
    and polled status.
  setup: >-
    1. Connect the projector's RS-232 port (or wire it to a serial bridge)
       with an RS-232 CROSS (null-modem) cable.
    2. Serial settings are 19200 baud, 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit,
       no flow control.
    3. Through a bridge, the binding's 19200 8N1 is applied to the line
       automatically.
  connection: >-
    The protocol requires a crossover (null-modem) cable between the
    controller/bridge and the projector. Status reads return on/standby,
    current input, lamp hours, internal temperatures, and the model name.

default_config:
  baudrate: 19200
  bytesize: 8
  parity: "N"
  stopbits: 1
  poll_interval: 15
  # The EK protocol requires >=200 ms between commands; keep a safe gap so
  # back-to-back commands and poll queries are not dropped.
  inter_command_delay: 0.25

config_schema:
  baudrate:
    type: integer
    default: 19200
    label: Baud Rate
    description: Fixed 19200 for the EK serial protocol.
  poll_interval:
    type: integer
    default: 15
    min: 0
    label: Poll Interval (sec)
    description: How often to poll power / input / lamp / temperature status. 0 disables polling.
  inter_command_delay:
    type: number
    default: 0.25
    min: 0.2
    label: Inter-Command Delay (sec)
    description: Minimum gap between commands. The protocol requires at least 0.2s.

state_variables:
  power:
    type: string
    label: Power
    help: >-
      Power state from CR0: On, Standby, Warming Up, Cooling, Power Failure,
      or a power-management / temperature-fault state. Unmapped codes show the
      raw two-character code.
  input:
    type: string
    label: Input Source
    help: "Current input from CR1 (e.g. VGA 1, HDMI 1, HDMI 2(MHL), Video, Network)."
  lamp_hours:
    type: integer
    label: Lamp Hours
    help: "Cumulative lamp-on hours from CR3."
  temperatures:
    type: string
    label: Temperatures (C)
    help: "Three internal temperature sensors (deg C) from CR6."
  model:
    type: string
    label: Model
    help: "Model name reported by CR5 (read once on connect, e.g. Coral-EK-307W)."

commands:
  # ── Power ──
  power_on:
    label: Power On
    send: "C00\r"
    help: Turn the projector on.
  power_off:
    label: Power Off
    send: "C01\r"
    help: >-
      Turn the projector off. Uses the immediate-off command (C01): a single
      press powers down with no on-screen confirmation, and the lamp still
      cools down normally. (The C02 "normal" off requires a front-panel
      confirmation and is not exposed; use Send Raw Command if you want it.)
  query_power:
    label: Query Power Status
    send: "CR0\r"

  # ── Input select ──
  input_vga1:
    label: "Input: VGA 1"
    send: "C05\r"
  input_vga2:
    label: "Input: VGA 2"
    send: "C06\r"
  input_video:
    label: "Input: Video"
    send: "C07\r"
  input_svideo:
    label: "Input: S-Video"
    send: "C34\r"
  input_component:
    label: "Input: Component"
    send: "C33\r"
  input_hdmi1:
    label: "Input: HDMI 1"
    send: "C36\r"
  input_hdmi2:
    label: "Input: HDMI 2 (MHL)"
    send: "C37\r"
  input_network:
    label: "Input: Network"
    send: "C15\r"
  input_memory_viewer:
    label: "Input: Memory Viewer"
    send: "C16\r"
  input_usb_display:
    label: "Input: USB Display"
    send: "C17\r"
  query_input:
    label: Query Input
    send: "CR1\r"

  # ── Picture ──
  blank_on:
    label: Blank On
    send: "C0D\r"
  blank_off:
    label: Blank Off
    send: "C0E\r"
  freeze_on:
    label: Freeze On
    send: "C43\r"
  freeze_off:
    label: Freeze Off
    send: "C44\r"
  display_clear:
    label: Clear On-Screen Display
    send: "C1E\r"
  image_toggle:
    label: Image Mode (Toggle)
    send: "C27\r"
  image_dynamic:
    label: "Image Mode: Dynamic"
    send: "C19\r"
  image_standard:
    label: "Image Mode: Standard"
    send: "C11\r"
  image_cinema:
    label: "Image Mode: Cinema"
    send: "C13\r"
  image_colorboard:
    label: "Image Mode: Colorboard"
    send: "C39\r"
  image_blackboard:
    label: "Image Mode: Blackboard"
    send: "C18\r"
  screen_normal:
    label: "Screen: Normal"
    send: "C0F\r"
  screen_wide:
    label: "Screen: Wide (16:9)"
    send: "C10\r"
  brightness_up:
    label: Brightness +
    send: "C20\r"
  brightness_down:
    label: Brightness -
    send: "C21\r"

  # ── Audio ──
  mute_on:
    label: Mute On
    send: "C0B\r"
  mute_off:
    label: Mute Off
    send: "C0C\r"
  volume_up:
    label: Volume +
    send: "C09\r"
  volume_down:
    label: Volume -
    send: "C0A\r"

  # ── Zoom / keystone ──
  digital_zoom_in:
    label: Digital Zoom +
    send: "C30\r"
  digital_zoom_out:
    label: Digital Zoom -
    send: "C31\r"
  keystone_up:
    label: Keystone +
    send: "C8E\r"
    help: Keystone adjustment. Exact axis varies by model.
  keystone_down:
    label: Keystone -
    send: "C8F\r"
    help: Keystone adjustment. Exact axis varies by model.
  keystone_alt_up:
    label: Keystone + (alt axis)
    send: "C90\r"
    help: Second keystone axis on models with horizontal keystone.
  keystone_alt_down:
    label: Keystone - (alt axis)
    send: "C91\r"
    help: Second keystone axis on models with horizontal keystone.

  # ── On-screen navigation ──
  menu_on:
    label: "Menu: On"
    send: "C1C\r"
  menu_off:
    label: "Menu: Off"
    send: "C1D\r"
  nav_up:
    label: "Navigate: Up"
    send: "C3C\r"
  nav_down:
    label: "Navigate: Down"
    send: "C3D\r"
  nav_left:
    label: "Navigate: Left"
    send: "C3B\r"
  nav_right:
    label: "Navigate: Right"
    send: "C3A\r"
  nav_enter:
    label: "Navigate: Enter"
    send: "C3F\r"
  auto_pc_adjust:
    label: Auto PC Adjust
    send: "C89\r"
  timer:
    label: Timer
    send: "C8A\r"
  timer_exit:
    label: Timer (Exit)
    send: "C8B\r"

  # ── Power management ──
  power_mgmt_ready:
    label: "Power Mgmt: Ready"
    send: "C2A\r"
  power_mgmt_off:
    label: "Power Mgmt: Off"
    send: "C2B\r"
  power_mgmt_shutdown:
    label: "Power Mgmt: Shut Down"
    send: "C2E\r"
  direct_on_enable:
    label: Direct Power On (Enable)
    send: "C28\r"
  direct_on_disable:
    label: Direct Power On (Disable)
    send: "C29\r"

  # ── Status reads ──
  query_lamp_hours:
    label: Query Lamp Hours
    send: "CR3\r"
  query_temperatures:
    label: Query Temperatures
    send: "CR6\r"
  query_model:
    label: Query Model Name
    send: "CR5\r"

  # ── Raw escape hatch ──
  send_command:
    label: Send Raw Command
    send: "{raw}\r"
    params:
      raw:
        type: string
        label: Raw Command
        description: >-
          Full command code without the carriage return, e.g. "C00" or "CR0".
          The driver appends the trailing CR. Use for codes not exposed above.

# Status replies are bare values (no echo of the query), so each pattern is
# anchored and the formats are mutually exclusive; the runtime stops at the
# first match. Power is the only 2-digit reply polled, so a 2-digit value is
# unambiguously the power code; lamp hours are 5 digits; temperatures lead
# with a decimal; the model is the only "Coral-" reply; any other text is the
# input name. The ACK (0x06) and "?" replies match nothing and are ignored.
responses:
  - match: '^(\d{2})$'
    mappings:
      - group: 1
        state: power
        map:
          "00": "On"
          "80": "Standby"
          "81": "Standby (Cooled)"
          "40": "Warming Up"
          "20": "Cooling"
          "21": "Cooling (Lamp Off)"
          "10": "Power Failure"
          "28": "Cooling (Temp Fault)"
          "88": "Recovering (Temp Fault)"
          "24": "Cooling (Power Mgmt)"
          "04": "Power Mgmt Ready"
  - match: '^(\d{4,6})$'
    set: { lamp_hours: "$1" }
  - match: '^\s*(\d+\.\d[\d.\s]*)$'
    set: { temperatures: "$1" }
  - match: '^(Coral-.+)$'
    set: { model: "$1" }
  - match: '^([A-Za-z].*)$'
    set: { input: "$1" }

polling:
  queries:
    - "CR0\r"
    - "CR1\r"
    - "CR3\r"
    - "CR6\r"

# Read the (static) model name once on connect.
on_connect:
  - "CR5\r"
